<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hoffmann-La Roche</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1896">1896</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Roche</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hoffmann-La Roche, usually known as Roche, is a Swiss pharmaceutical and diagnostics company founded in Basel in 1896 by Fritz Hoffmann-La Roche. It grew out of the Basel chemical tradition into one of the largest pharmaceutical firms in the world, known for products such as Valium and, later, cancer medicines and diagnostic tests.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hoffmann-la-roche.jpg" alt="Hoffmann-La Roche" width="320" height="167" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: F. Hoffmann-La Roche AG / Pentagram · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:F._Hoffmann-La_Roche_2021_logo.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>F. Hoffmann-La Roche, commonly known as Roche, was founded in 1896 by Fritz Hoffmann-La Roche and has its headquarters in Basel. It was known early on for various vitamin preparations and their derivatives, and in 1934 it became the first company to mass-produce synthetic vitamin C, under the brand name Redoxon. Descendants of the founding Hoffmann and Oeri families own slightly over half of the voting bearer shares.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>During the Second World War Roche is reported to have collaborated with the Nazi regime and to have used forced labour in its German and Polish factories; it also moved all its Jewish employees to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. In 1957 it introduced the benzodiazepines, among them Valium and Rohypnol.</p><p>In 1973 Stanley Adams, a manager in Basel, gave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-economic-community">European Economic Community</a> evidence of price fixing and market sharing for vitamins; he was imprisoned under Swiss law, and in 1999 the firm pleaded guilty to a worldwide conspiracy to fix vitamin prices and paid 500 million dollars in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> fines. In 1976 an accident at a chemical factory at Seveso in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, owned by a Roche subsidiary, caused a large dioxin contamination.</p><p>Roche bought the patents for the polymerase chain reaction technique in 1992, acquired Syntex in 1994 and Chugai Pharmaceuticals in 2002, and in 1998 bought Corange, parent of Boehringer Mannheim, which became Roche Diagnostics. In 1995 the United States FDA approved its HIV protease inhibitor saquinavir. In 2009 it acquired Genentech, in which it had held a majority stake since 1990, for 46.8 billion dollars.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Roche operates two divisions, Pharmaceuticals and Diagnostics. Roche Diagnostics makes equipment and reagents for research and medical diagnosis, and was organised into five business areas: Applied Science, Professional Diagnostics, Diabetes Care, Molecular Diagnostics and Tissue Diagnostics. The holding company, Roche Holding, has shares listed on the SIX Swiss Exchange. Roche also owns Genentech as a wholly owned independent subsidiary and the Japanese firm Chugai.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Roche” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Engineering firms such as Escher Wyss in Zurich built turbines, locomotives and steamships, while dye works in Basel grew into a chemical and pharmaceutical industry that included <b>Hoffmann-La Roche</b>, founded in 1896.</blockquote><a href="/empire/swiss-confederation">Swiss Confederation · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the de la <b>Roche</b> family the duchy prospered, with its court at Thebes, a silk industry and a Latin church that turned the Parthenon into a Catholic cathedral of Our Lady.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-athens">Duchy of Athens</a></li></ul></section>
